  |title I can't stand any degree of inauthenticity even in something wildly fictional

  |quote
    While I haven't honed the craft to any degree, I do love it. I find it fascinating though that I still can't stand any degree of inauthenticity even in something clearly, wildly fictional. The best stories are those that are so deeply honest that they are revealing things that the author couldn't have even known... tapping into those deep, ancient currents of truth that lie just beyond, obscured in the infinity of meaningful and plausible but, unlike meaningful and plausible, never fading and never perishing...

  |occasion
    Replying to an ELI's appreciation of his fiction writing, and turning it into a
    statement about why authenticity is not a genre convention.

  |about
    The clearest statement of what *truth* means to him in a register where nothing is
    literally true — and it is not accuracy. The best writing is honest enough to reveal
    what the author did not know, and the distinction that carries the weight is between
    *meaningful and plausible* (infinite, fading, perishable) and what lies beyond it.
    That is the same distinction his epistemic corrections keep pointing at, arrived at
    from the aesthetic side.

  |before-acting
    - You are writing prose whose value depends on the reader believing it is real.
    - You are producing something plausible and meaningful and have not asked whether it
      is true.
    - You are about to reach for a fine phrase.
    - You are adopting a voice you think will land rather than one that is yours.

  |see
    - #exm-hypothesis-is-not-truth
    - #exm-wisdom-strength-and-beauty

  |working-notes
    Register note: this is the aesthetic twin of #exm-hypothesis-is-not-truth —
    *meaningful and plausible* is the same category he refuses to let confidence
    promote.

